ABSTRACT In this work, we demonstrate cerium (Ce) based metal-organic-frameworks (MOFs) combined with carbon nanotubes (CNTs) to form Ce-MOF/CNT composites as separator coating material in Li-S battery system, which showed excellent electrochemical performance even under high sulfur loading, and much better capacity retention. At the sulfur loading of $2.5~\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , initial specific capacity of $1021.8\:\mathrm{mAh/g}$ at 1C was achieved in the Li-S cell with the Ce-MOF-2/CNT coated separator, which was slowly reduced to $838.8~\mathsf{m A h/g}$ after 800 cycles with a decay rate of only $0.022\%$ and the coulomb efficiency of nearly $100\%$ . Even at a higher sulfur loading of $6\:\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the cell based on Ce-MOF-2/CNT separator coating still exhibited excellent performance with initial specific capacity of $993.5\ \mathrm{mAh/g}$ at 0.1 C. After 200 cycles, the specific capacity of $886.4~\mathsf{m A h/g}$ was still retained. The excellent performance is ascribed to the efficient adsorption of the Ce-MOF-2 to $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ species and its catalytic effect towards conversion of polysulfides, resulting in suppressed shuttle effect of ploysulfides in the LiS batteries
KEYWORDS: Li-S battery, metal organic frameworks, separator coating, catalytic conversion, Cerium
Nowadays, with the increasing demand of energy for portable electronic devices and electric vehicles, it is urgent to develop high energy density energy storage devices such as batteries. While traditional lithium-ion batteries have achieved enormous success as energy storage devices, their shortage of limited theoretical capacity calls for development of alternative energy storage systems that bear advantage of high energy density and safety.  Due to the high theoretical specific capacity $(1675\ \mathsf{m A h/g})$ , high theoretical energy density (2600 Wh/Kg) as well as the abundant natural reserves and low cost of sulfur element, lithium sulfur (Li-S) battery has been considered as one of the promising next generation high energy storage systems.1 However, the practical applications of Li-S batteries are still hindered by obstacles, such as unsatisfactory energy density and poor capacity retention of the device due to (i) the low utilization of active materials because of the insulating nature of sulfur and lithium sulfide $(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}/\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ ; (ii) the damage of electrode structure caused by volume change of sulfur and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ , (iii) the shuttle effect caused by the solution of lithium polysulfide during the charging and discharging process.2-4 In recent years, many strategies have been developed to solve the above issues to improve the performance of Li-S batteries, including using porous materials like carbons, metal oxides and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as the host for sulfur cathodes, designing multifunctional binders, adding functional interlayer between the separator and cathode and so on.5-11 Among them, the separator as an important component of battery is responsible for separating cathode and anode to avoid short-circuiting while allowing transport of conducting ions in the electrolyte during the operation of the batteries. In the system of Li-S batteries, polysulfides may also pass through the separator reaching to the lithium anode. Hence tailoring properties of the separator could be an effective way to hinder the shuttle effect of polysulfides, thus improving performance of Li-S battery.12-18 Strategies including using free-standing functional interlayer and separator coating layer have been reported. In particular, use of a separator coating layer is considered to be more advantageous due to the simple preparation process, stable structure and lightness of material coating layer. It is generally known that porous carbon materials such as graphene and carbon nanotubes (CNT) have been widely used as separator coating materials owing to their excellent electrical conductivity. They can not only block the polysulfides, but also act as secondary charge collector to promote electric conduction inside the battery.12,13 Nonetheless, the non-polar surface of CNTs  means they have weak affinity for polysulfides. Therefore they can only physically block the polysulfide shuttle, which is quite often inefficient. Attempts of using materials coating with catalytic effect on polysulfides (for instance metal oxides, sulfides and nitrides, etc.) have been reported on the surface of conductive porous carbon to improve blocking effect of polysulfides.14-
18 On the one hand, the polysulfides could be catalyzed by the coating materials and transformed into short chain lithium sulfides, reducing the shuttle of polysulfides and improving the reaction kinetics of the electrode. On the other hand, the porous carbon could enhance the conductivity of materials and thus the utilization rate of the active materials. Therefore, a rational design and development of effective methods for fabrication of functional separator coating materials, which possess both efficiently catalytic capability for the conversion of polysulfides and good electrical conductivity, is crucial to further improve the electrochemical performance of Li-S batteries. Constituted by organic ligands and inorganic metal ions, metal-organic-frameworks (MOFs) have been widely used in the fields of separation, sensing, catalysis and electrochemistry due to their large specific surface area, flexibility to design MOF with tunable pore sizes and chemical propeties.19-21 Recently, using MOFs and their derivatives as the cathode host for Li-S batteries have attracted lots of attention. $22-25|{\mathsf{n}}$ comparison, reports on MOFs as functional separator coating for Li-S batteries are rare. Furthermore, the limited research of MOF based separator reported previously is based on the sieving effect of MOFs to selectively separate the polysulfide and lithium ions.26-
28 Because it is difficult to coat MOFs particles on the separator to form a continuous and compact coating layer, the polysulfide ions could easily penetrate through the areas without MOF coating. This greatly restricts the sieving effect of the MOFs.
It has been reported that the open metal sites of MOFs could serve as catalytic active sites for many reactions.29-31 Compared with the traditional inorganic porous catalysts or nano catalysts, MOFs possess better catalytic performance due to the uniformly dispersed catalytic sites and larger specific surface area. Inspired by a recent report,5 in which the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles can adsorb and catalyze the conversion of polysulfides well, herein we synthesized MOFs containing ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ -cluster nodes which were then combined with CNT to form Ce-MOFs/CNT composites which was used as the separator coating for Li-S battery. We have found that the Ce-MOF-2 which possessed a large specific surface area and coordination-unsaturated Ce(IV)-cluster nodes could quickly adsorb the polysulfides and effectively catalyze their conversion. By combining the high conductivity of CNT, the as-synthesized Ce-MOF-2/CNT composite demonstrated excellent electrochemical and cycling performance of Li-S battery with outstanding capacity retention. The Ce-MOF-2/CNT porous separator coating material (Figure 1a) plays the dual functional roles of catalytic conversion of polysulfides and blocking the transport path of polysulfides, thus effectively suppressing the shuttle effect in Li-S batteries. This work provides an effective strategy to improve the performance of lithium-sulfur batteries.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
To verify the adsorption effect of open metal sites for polysulfides, the Ce-MOFs with different open metal sites were designed including Ce-MOF-1 (also called Ce-UiO-66- BPDC) and Ce-MOF-2 (also called Ce-MOF-808).32 The metal Ce(IV)-cluster nodes of these two Ce-MOFs are both hexanuclear clusters (Figure 1b) but have different coordination number of carboxyl ligands. The hexanuclear ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ clusters $\mathsf{L C e}_{6}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathsf{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\mathsf{-O H}})_{4}({\mathsf{-C O}}_{2})_{12}]$ in Ce-MOF-1 are completely surrounded by twelve carboxyl groups while the corresponding hexanuclear Ce(IV) clusters $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C e}_{6}(\mathsf{\upmu}_{3}{\mathsf{-O}})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\mathsf{-O H}})_{4}(-$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{6}]$ in Ce-MOF-2 are coordinated with only six carboxyl groups, leaving six unsaturated coordination sites for the adsorption of polysulfides. Density functional theory (DFT) calculation was used to study the adsorption sites between the hexanuclear metal clusters and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ . As shown in Figure 1b, the distance between terminal S of $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ and the ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ is about 3.291Å due to large steric hindrance derived from the coordination saturation around the hexanuclear clusters in Ce-MOF-1. For CeMOF-2, thanks to the unsaturated coordination sites of hexanuclear clusters, the terminal S of $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ is closer to the ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ sites with distance about 2.304Å. Meanwhile the lithium ion can also interact with the coordination hydroxyl oxygen. These lead to larger binding energy $(\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{b}})$ between $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ and the clusters in Ce-MOF-2 $(\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{b}}=2.48\ \mathsf{e V})$ compared to the Ce-MOF-1 $(\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{b}}=1.23\ \mathsf{e V})$ . It indicates that Ce-MOFs-2 with the open metal sites could adsorb the polysulfides more effectively than the Ce-MOF-1. In addition, investigation of the interaction between Ce-MOF-2 and other intermediate polysulfides, such as $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ , $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ and ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}$ shows there is also strong binding force between the intermediates with the Ce-MOF-2. Binding energy values of 2.78 eV for $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ , 2.65 eV for $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{3}$ and $2.35\:\mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}$ are predicted by DFT calculation (Figure S1). This suggests the polysulfides can be effectively immobilized by the Ce-MOF-2. 33
The Ce-MOFs-1,2/CNT composites were synthesized by refluxing the mixture of diammonium cerium nitrate, biphenyl-4,4'-dicarboxylic acid and CNT in DMF (See the details in Experimental section). The XRD spectrum of the obtained Ce-MOF-1/CNT and Ce-MOF-2/CNT match well with the corresponding simulated peaks respectively, indicating the pure phase of the as-synthesized samples (Figure 2a). The SEM (Figure 2 c-d) and TEM images (Figure S2 a-c) show that Ce-MOF-1 is composed of cubic nanoparticles of about 350 nm which are connected with each other through carbon nanotubes to form Ce-MOF-1/CNT composite, while the Ce-MOF-2 shows the nanoparticles with smaller size of 180 nm that grew along the CNTs (Figure 2e-f, Figure S2 d-f). The specific surface area of Ce-MOF-1/CNT, Ce-MOF-2/CNT and CNT were tested by nitrogen adsorption isotherm under 77K. The results (Figure 2b) reveal that their Langmuir specific surface area is $1038.4~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ for Ce-MOF-1/CNT, $947.6~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ for Ce-MOF-2/CNT and $546.8~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ for CNTs, respectively. In order to further verify the interaction between the synthesized materials and the polysulfides, the active Ce-MOFs/CNT composites and CNTs were added to the ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{6}$ solution. As revealed in Figure 3a, after 5 minutes, the color of the solution with CeMOF-1/CNT became slightly lighter than that with CNT, which may be due to the stronger interaction between $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ and hexanuclear clusters of Ce-MOF-1 than that with carbon. In contrast, the interaction between $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ and Ce-MOF-2/CNT, in which its metal sites are only partially occupied by the carboxyl groups, is much stronger than that of Ce-MOF-1/CNT. This is also reflected by the color change of the solution in which the color of $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ solution containing Ce-MOF-2/CNT became colorless within 5 min while Ce-MOF-1/CNT still shows light yellow color, indicating that the open metal sites in Ce-MOF-2/CNT could adsorb $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ more efficiently. We further tested the thermal stability of the Ce-MOFs and Ce-MOFs/CNTs. As shown in Figure S3 and S4, the thermal behaviors of Ce-MOF-1 and Ce-MOF-2 are similar to their corresponding composites of Ce-MOF-1/CNTs and Ce-MOF-2/CNTs with a slight loss of weight with increase of temperature at low temperature below $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . It suggests addition of CNTs does not affect the thermal properties of the Ce-MOFs. Dramatic weight loss at temperature above $300~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ is noticed with all the Ce-MOFs based samples due to decomposition of the Ce-MOFs. Based on the thermal analysis, we also determined the weight ratio of Ce-MOFs in the composites. There is $86.8\%$ Ce-MOF-1 in the Ce-MOF1/CNTs and $86.2\%$ Ce-MOF-2 in the Ce-MOF-2/CNTs, respectively.
The XPS measurement for Ce-MOFs/CNT composites and CNT after adsorbing ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{6}$ , named as Ce-MOF-1/CNT@Li2S6, Ce-MOF-2/CNT@Li2S6 and CNT@Li2S6, were performed to explore their interaction with $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ (Figure 3b). We analyzed the XPS spectrum of S 2p of the three samples. For the CN $\begin{array}{r}{{\mathsf{I T@L i_{2}S_{6}}},}\end{array}$ , the peaks of S $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ at 161.7eV and 163.2eV are attributed to the terminal S $(\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{T}})$ and bridging S $(S_{\mathsf{B}})$ , respectively, while both XPS peaks of S $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ for Ce-MOF-1/CNT $@\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ are upshifted by $0.4~\mathsf{e V}$ , indicating that the interaction of Ce-MOF-1 with $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ , which is also proved by the visual adsorption experiments shown in Fig. 3(a). For the Ce-MOF-2/CNT@Li2S6, the peaks of $\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{T}}$ and $S_{\mathsf{B}}$ are further upshifted compared to Ce-MOF-1/CNT@Li2S6 as a result of stronger interaction with $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ . Furthermore, compared with Ce-MOF$1/\mathsf{C N T}@\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ and CN $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{T@L i_{2}}}\mathsf{S_{6}}$ , two additional peaks at 167.3eV and $168.8\mathsf{e V}$ in the S $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ are observed with Ce-MOF-2/CNT@Li2S6, which could be related to the formation of species such as thiosulfate and polythionate due to the redox reaction between CeMOF-2 and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ .15
To further confirm the catalytic effect of Ce-MOF-2 on polysulfides $(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6})$ conversion, the synthesized Ce-MOFs/CNT composites and bare CNT were coated on an aluminum foil respectively which was then assembled into symmetrical batteries with or without $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ for electrochemical testing. Figure 3c shows the chronoamperometry curves of the symmetrical batteries. With the addition of $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ in the electrolyte, all the symmetrical batteries show the increased current compared with those without $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ . This indicates that the current generated during the lithium/delithium process is responsible for the majority of the current in the symmetrical battery, rather than the space double-layer.18 The electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) of the cells are shown in Figure 3d. The charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , which reflects the reaction kinetics of the electrode material, of Ce-MOF-1/CNT and CNT are similar, while that of Ce-MOF-2/CNT is much smaller. This suggests that Ce-MOF-2/CNT could effectively enhance the reaction kinetics process and promote the conversion of polysulfides.18 Moreover, the CV curves show that the current response of Ce-MOF-2/CNT symmetrical battery with $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ is much higher than that of Ce-MOF-1/CNT and CNT, and there is no obvious reaction current for all the symmetrical batteries without $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ as manifested with nearly zero current (Figure 3e). This demonstrates that the Ce-MOF-2/CNT has the beneficial effect of immobilizing the polysulfides $(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6})$ and facilitating the redox reaction of polysulfides.18
The results of DFT calculation, polysulfides adsorption experiment together with the electrochemical tests of the symmetric batteries shown above indicate that Ce-MOF2/CNT could be a promising separator coating material for Li-S battery. Thus we made a slurry composed of the Ce-MOF-2/CNT, super P as conductive agent and LA132 binder. The slurry was coated on a Celgard separator to prepare the Ce-MOF-2/CNT modified separator (Figure S5). As shown in Figure S5 (a-d), the Ce-MOF-2/CNT can be coated on the Celgard separator to form a uniform coating layer with thickness of about ${8\upmu\mathsf{m}}$ . The surface of the Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating layer has porous structure and retains similar morphology with the synthesized Ce-MOF-2/CNT composite powder as shown in Figure 2(e-f), which could facilitate the transport of electrolyte ions and Li+. This is in contrast to the bare Celgard separator which has lots of large voids in the surface (Figures S6). The modified separator was then assembled into coin-type Li-S cells with the coating layer facing the cathode for electrochemical testing. The cyclic voltammetry curves of all cells shown in Figure 4a exhibit two obvious reduction peaks at 2.3 V and $\mathord{\sim}2.0\mathrm{~V~}$ , corresponding to the transformation from $\mathsf{S}_{8}$ to long-chain polysulfides and the further reduction of polysulfides to $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}/\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ respectively,18 while the two peaks of the oxidation process correspond to the reverse process of transformation from Li2S2/Li2S to polysulfides and finally to S8. Compared with the pristine Celgard separator, the reduction peaks of the cells with CNT and Ce-MOF1/CNT coating are positively shifted slightly, indicating that CNT coating could improve the polarization of the cells. This is because the conductive CNT could serve as a secondary charge collector to improve the battery performance. For the Ce-MOF-1/CNT composite, because the adsorption effect between Ce-MOF-1 and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ is not strong, the CV plot of Ce-MOF-1/ CNT is similar to that of CNT though the peak intensity is slightly higher. In contrast, the reduction peaks in the CV plot of Ce-MOF-2/CNT membrane coating display distinguishable positive shift as a result of effectively adsorption and catalytic effect for the conversion of polysulfides to reduce the polarization. This is also proved by the minimum value of voltage change △V (lowest voltage hysteresis) of the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating in the charge-discharge curve under the current density of 0.1C. $\triangle\lor=107.8~\mathrm{mV}$ for Ce-MOF-2/CNT, which is much lower than that of Ce-MOF-1/CNT coating $\langle\Delta V=165.2\mathsf{m V})$ , CNT coating ( $\triangle\lor=$ $172.4\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mV}})$ and Celgard $\begin{array}{r l}{(\triangle\lor=}&{{}201.9\mathrm{~mV})}\end{array}$ as shown in Figure 4b. The largely unchanged CV curves of the Li-S cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT separator coating at different cycles in Figure S7 proves the stability of the Ce-MOF-2/CNT during the electrochemical process.
Figure 4c shows the rate performance of the cells with various separators under the sulfur loading of $2.5\ \mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . The specific capacities of the cells with Ce-MOFs/CNT composites or CNT coating under different current are higher than that of the Celgard. For the cell with Ce-MOF-1/CNT coating, the stronger polysulfides adsorption ability enables the coating layer to block the shuttle of polysulfides more effectively than CNT coating, leading to higher specific capacities under different current densities. For the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating, it has a stable specific capacity under each current density, which is much higher than that of Celgard, CNT or Ce-MOF-1 /CNT coating. The specific capacity of the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating is 1140.7 mAh/g, 1066.3 mAh/g, 977.4 mAh/g and 802.8 mAh/g at 0.2C, 0.5C, 1C and 2C, respectively. The CeMOF-2/CNT coating based cell shows 662.6 mAh/g even at a large discharge current of 4C. Moreover, the specific capacity is restored to 1095.1 mAh/g when the current was returned from 4C to 0.2C. The charge-discharge curves of the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating at various rates also reveal its good rate performance (Figure 4d). The cycling stabilities of the cells based on different separator coatings at 1C with the sulfur loading of $2.5~\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ are shown in Figure 4e. The initial specific capacity of the cell with CeMOF-2/CNT coating is as high as $1021.8~\mathsf{m A h/g}$ , which is slowly reduced to 838.8 mAh/g after 800 cycles with a decay rate of $0.022\%$ and the coulomb efficiency is nearly $100\%$ . In contrast, the capacity of Ce-MOF-1/CNT based cell decreases from 701.8 to 349.4 mAh/g and CNT based cell from 622.9 to 237.6 mAh/g after 800 cycles.
Apparently, the capacity retention of the Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating based cell is much better than that of Ce-MOF-1/CNT and CNT.
To further understand the effect of the different separator coating materials on cell performance, we disassembled the cell after the cycling testing. As shown in Figure S8, among all the samples, the Li anode of the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNTs coating suffered the least damage. Comparison of the SEM images of the Li anodes (Figure S9-12) shows that the surface of the Li anode was the coarsest in the cell with bare Celgard separator (Figure S9). While the use of the CNT (Figure S10) or Ce-MOF-1/CNT (Figure S11) separator coating suppresses the formation of corrosion and formation of dendrites at a certain degree, the damage of Li surface is still very significant.  In contrast, the surface of the Li anode with Ce-MOF-2/CNT separator coating is still smooth and dense even after 800 charge/discharge cycling test (Figure S12). Furthermore, we investigated the chemical composition of the Li anode by EDS mapping. The results show the content of S in the lithium anode of the Ce-MOF-2/CNT based cell is the lowest among the samples investigated (Table S1). The S in the Li anode is ascribed to the insoluble Li2S2/Li2S deposited on the anode surface.34 To further check the stability of Ce-MOF-2/CNT, the XRD of cycled MOF separator modified were measured. As shown in Figure S13, the X-ray diffraction peaks of the CeMOF-2/CNT after cycling testing still match well with those of the fresh coating without experiencing cycling test. All these results confirm that Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating with highly efficient adsorption and catalysis for polysulfides could effectively inhibit the shuttle of polysulfides and improve the cycling stability of the Li-S batteries. Even in a higher sulfur loading of 6 mg/cm2, the cell with Ce-MOF-2/CNT coating could still possess excellent electrochemical performance with initial specific capacity of 993.5 mAh/g. After 200 cycles, specific capacity of $886.4~\mathsf{m A h/g}$ is retained with nearly $100\%$ coulomb efficiency (Figure 4f). In order to further understand the influence of the coating thickness on the electrochemical properties of the batteries, we prepared separators with thinner CeMOF-2/CNT coating layer of about ${\sim}6.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and ${\sim}3.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S14 a,b) and tested the performance of these separators in Li-S batteries. Comparison of the electrochemical cycling performance of the Li-S battery with different separator coating thickness at current density of 1.0 C and 0.1 C (Figure S14 (c,d)) shows that when the coating thickness decreases from ${\sim}8~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to ${\sim}6.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the specific capacity is slightly reduced while the stability is maintained. It suggests that the polysulfide shuttle could still be effectively hindered with the ${\sim}6.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ coating thickness. However, when the thickness further decreases to ${\sim}3.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the specific capacity and the stability are dramatically reduced, suggesting the coating layer is too thin to effectively block the transport of polysulfides in the cell. Although MOFs have been reported as separator coating in Li-S batteries, all the MOFs reported previously functioned through the physical sieving effect of MOFs by taking advantage of the suitable pore size to selectively separate the polysulfide and lithium ions.22-25 Because of the difficulty in achieving continuous and compact coating  of MOFs particles on the separator, the polysulfide ions normally easily penetrate through the gap among the particles, resulting in unsatisfactory reduction of shuttle effect associated with polysulfides. We compared the electrochemical performance of Li-S battery using representative separator reported in literature including specific capacity retention, fading rate and surfur loading  with our work (Table S2). Clearly, the active sulfur loading in previous reports is much lower and the resulted areal capacities are not high too. For example, the capacity of the cell with HKUST(Cu)@GO based separator was 0.256 mAh/g (855 mAh/g) after 800 cycles at 1C with the sulfur loading of only 0.3 mg/cm2, while the Li-S battery using Y-FTZB/glass fiber-based separator showed specific capacity of 0.557 mAh/g (557 mAh/g) after 300 cycles at 0.25C with the sulfur loading of only 1 mg/cm2.26,28 The extraordinary electrochemical performance of the CeMOF-2/CNT coating in this work is attributed to the synergistic effect of efficient adsorption and catalytic conversion of Ce-MOF-2 for polysulfides together with the high conductive CNT, which works well even at high sulfur loading.
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, this research demonstrates that Ce-MOF-2 with large specific surface area, highly dispersed and catalytically active open metal sites could effectively enhance the adsorption and catalytic conversion of polysulfides, which could be employed as the separator coating to effectively inhibit the shuttle effect in Li-S batteries, leading to excellent electrochemical performance with the batteries. Our work provides an effective strategy for exploring MOFs based materials for high-performance lithiumsulfur batteries.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Preparation of Ce-MOFs-1,2/CNT Composites
The carbon nanotubes were actived before being used. To synthesize the Ce-MOF1/CNT, 0.50 g biphenyl-4,4'-dicarboxylic acid, 60 mg actived CNT and $30~\mathsf{m L}$ DMF were mixed together and sonicated for 30min. 2.90 g ceric ammonium nitrate was dispered in 10 mL water and then added to the prepared solution and heated at $100\%$ for 30 min. The resulted complexes were rinsed with DMF and ethanol for 2 times and dried at 60 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 2h to obtain Ce-MOF-1/CNT. To make the Ce-MOF-2/CNT compisite, 0.56 g benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylic acid, 60 mg actived CNT and 30 mL DMF were first mixed together and sonicated for 30 min. After this, 4.50 g ceric ammonium nitrate was dispered in $15~\mathsf{m L}$ water and then added to the prepared solution and heated at $100\%$ for 30 min. The resulted complexes were rinsed with DMF and ethanol for 2 times and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2h$ to get Ce-MOF-2/CNT.
## Preparation of Ce-MOFs/CNT and CNT Coated Separators
The Ce-MOFs/CNT and CNT coated separators were prepared by the same method. Briefly, 10 wt% LA132 binder, 80 wt% Ce-MOFs/CNT composite or CNT and $10\mathrm{\mt\%}$ super-P were mixed in isopropanol to form a slurry which was then coated on separator (Celgard 2400). Then, the slurry-coated separator was dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $18{\mathfrak{h}}$ under vacuum. After dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $18{\mathfrak{h}}$ under vacuum, the obtained CeMOFs/CNT or CNT coated separator was punched into circular disks with a mass loading of $0.4\:\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for battery assembly.
## Preparation of Sulfur Cathodes
Sulfur cathodes were prepared using a slurry casting method. For electrodes with sulfur loading of 2.5 mg/cm2, 10 wt% LA132 binder, 10 wt% super-P and 80 wt% Ketjen Black/sulfur composite were mixed in n-propyl alcohol to form a slurry which was then coated on an aluminum current collector as cathode and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ over $18\mathsf{h}$ . To make electrodes with sulfur loading of $6\:\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , Ketjen Black/sulfur composite, carbon nanofiber, super-P and LA132 binder were mixed with a weight ratio of 88:4:1:7 in npropyl alcohol to form a slurry that was then coated on an aluminum current collectors and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ over $24\mathsf{h}$ .
## Electrochemical Measurements
Coin cells 2032 were assembled in an Ar-filled glovebox ( $\mathrm{\tilde{O}}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}<0.1$ ppm) with an electrolyte of 1.0 M LiTFSI and $0.1\mathrm{~M~LiNO}_{3}$ in DOL/DME (v:v, 1:1), Celgard 2400, CeMOFs/CNT or CNT coated separators, and Li foil reference anode. About 40 μL electrolyte was added for each coin cell. Galvanostatic charge/discharge was carried out between 1.7 and $2.8\:\vee$ using LAND CT2001A instrument (Wuhan Jinnuo Electronic Co. Ltd.) at different C rates $(10=1680\:\mathrm{mA/g)}$ . The electrodes were used contained no elemental sulfur for symmetrical cells. The CeMOFs/CNT composites or CNT and PVDF with a weight ratio of 8:2 were dispersed together in N-methylpyrrolidone (NMP) to form the slurry and then coated on Al foil and dried in vacuum at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The obtained pieces were used as the same working and counter electrodes after punched into circular disks. The Li2S and S with the ratio of 1:5 were added to prepare the electrolyte contained 0.5M $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6}$ . For the symmetrical cells, the CV measurements were carried out at the rate of $50~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\thinspace\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in the range of $-0.5{\sim}0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ . At the open circuit potential, the frequency was set between 100 kHz and 0.01 Hz for the EIS measurements. The chronoamperometry curves were tested with the pulsed potentials between −0.5 V (60 s) and 0.5 V(60 s).
## Characterization
The X-ray powder diffraction patterns of the samples were measured on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer at $40~\mathsf{k V}$ and 40 mA with a Cu target tube and a graphite monochromator. Automatic volumetric adsorption equipment (Belsorp-max) was used to test the samples’ nitrogen sorpitn. The SEM morphology was measured using TESCAN Brno, s.r.o. MAIA3 LMH field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Thermo Scientific Escalab 250Xi instrument with Mg Kα radiation (1253.6 eV) was used for Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis at a scan step of 0.1 eV.
## Computational Methods
Density functional theory (DFT) within the Perdew–Berke– Ernzerh of generalized gradient approximation (GGA-PBE) implementing in the Dmol3 package was used to calculate the binding energies between Ce(IV) hexanuclear clusters and polysulfide $(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6})$ . The atomic potentials was expressed by the double numerical plus polarization (DNP) basis sets with effective core potential and the SCF tolerance was set below $1\times10^{-6}$ . The adsorption energy between segment of ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ hexanuclear clusters and adsorbed polysulfide $(\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{6})$ is determined by $E_{\mathrm{a}}=E_{\mathrm{seg.}}+E_{\mathrm{Li2S6}}-E_{\mathrm{Li2S6}}@_{\mathsf{s e g.}}$ where ELi2S6, $\begin{array}{r}{E_{\mathsf{s e g.}}}\end{array}$ and ELi2S6 $@_{\mathsf{s e g}}$ . denote the total energies of the the adsorbed polysulfide, segment of ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathsf{N}})$ hexanuclear clusters, and the polysulfide - Ce(IV) hexanuclear clusters, respectively.
